<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE policyconfig PUBLIC
 "-//freedesktop//DTD PolicyKit Policy Configuration 1.0//EN"
 "http://www.freedesktop.org/standards/PolicyKit/1.0/policyconfig.dtd">

<!--
  This file is what lets RESONANCE put a file back into /etc or /usr.

  Installed to /usr/share/polkit-1/actions/. The annotation at the bottom is
  the whole mechanism: pkexec looks for an action whose exec.path matches the
  program it was asked to run, and uses that action's rules. Change the path
  and elevation stops working; it must stay in step with helperPath in
  elevate.go, which names the same location literally.

  Because polkit authorises a *path* and not a binary, a development build
  running out of build/bin/ cannot elevate at all. That is the design working,
  not a bug, and the app says so in plain words rather than failing at a
  prompt that never appears.

  auth_admin_keep, not auth_admin: restoring an app means putting back several
  files at once, and being asked once per file is not an app anyone would use.
  "keep" holds the authorisation for a few minutes — the behaviour of Dolphin's
  "act as administrator", which is the one that was asked for.

  The DOCTYPE above is polkit's required boilerplate, identical in every policy
  file on the system. Nothing fetches that URL.
-->
<policyconfig>

  <vendor>RESONANCE</vendor>
  <vendor_url>https://github.com/sudo-megas/RESONANCE</vendor_url>
  <icon_name>resonance</icon_name>

  <action id="xyz.namli.resonance.helper">
    <description>Put back system configuration files</description>
    <message>Authentication is required for RESONANCE to put files back into /etc and /usr</message>
    <defaults>
      <allow_any>auth_admin</allow_any>
      <allow_inactive>auth_admin</allow_inactive>
      <allow_active>auth_admin_keep</allow_active>
    </defaults>
    <annotate key="org.freedesktop.policykit.exec.path">/usr/lib/resonance/resonance-helper</annotate>
  </action>

</policyconfig>
